          "role": "user",
          "content": "Board: Arduino Nano 33 BLE Sense, Arduino mbed core\n\n<handout>\n# Lab 3: Hardware PWM (mock handout)\n\n> Mock handout written for testing `grader make-plan`. It is not an official course\n> document.\n\n## Goal\n\nGenerate a **1 kHz** square wave with a **25 % duty cycle** on pin **D5** of your\nArduino Nano 33 BLE Sense.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. The signal on D5 must have a frequency of 1 kHz and a duty cycle of 25 %.\n2. The signal must start on its own after reset. No serial input or button press is\n   needed.\n3. You must use one of the nRF52840's **hardware timers / PWM peripherals**. Solutions\n   that toggle the pin from `loop()` with `delay()` or `delayMicroseconds()`, or that use\n   `analogWrite()`, will lose points at the TA review.\n\n## Submission\n\nSubmit a single sketch folder containing your `.ino` file (and any `.cpp`/`.h` files it\nuses). Comment the register values or constants you chose and explain how you computed\nthem.\n\n## Hints\n\n* The PWM output must be stable soon after boot; the grader starts measuring shortly\n  after reset.\n* Check your prescaler and counter-top values carefully: being off by a factor of two is\n  the most common mistake.\n</handout>\n\n<requirements>\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pwm_frequency\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The signal on pin D5 must have a frequency of 1 kHz.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pwm_duty_cycle\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The signal on pin D5 must have a duty cycle of 25%.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"auto_start\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The PWM signal on D5 must start automatically after reset without any serial input or button press.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"stable_after_boot\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The PWM signal on D5 must be stable (correct frequency and duty cycle) within 50 ms after reset.\"\n  }\n]\n</requirements>\n\n<student_values>\nnone\n</student_values>\n\n<student_code untrusted=\"true\">\n=== submission_03.ino ===\n   1| // ECE Lab 3 - hardware PWM on D5\n   2| //\n   3| // D5 -> P1.13\n   4| // I compute the register values from the clock so I don't hardcode magic numbers.\n   5| //\n   6| //   CORE_CLK   = 64 MHz (nRF52840 runs at 64 MHz)\n   7| //   PRESCALER  = 16  -> 64 MHz / 16 = 4 MHz tick\n   8| //   TOP        = 4 MHz / 1 kHz = 4000\n   9| //   DUTY       = TOP / 4 = 1000  (25%)\n  10| //\n  11| // TOP fits in the 15-bit COUNTERTOP register (max 32767).\n  12| \n  13| #include <Arduino.h>\n  14| \n  15| #define CORE_CLK_HZ   64000000UL\n  16| #define PRESC_DIV     16UL\n  17| #define TARGET_HZ     1000UL\n  18| \n  19| #define PWM_TOP_VAL   (CORE_CLK_HZ / PRESC_DIV / TARGET_HZ)\n  20| #define PWM_DUTY_VAL  (PWM_TOP_VAL / 4)\n  21| \n  22| static uint16_t seq_values[] = { (uint16_t)(PWM_DUTY_VAL | 0x8000) };\n  23| \n  24| void setup() {\n  25|   NRF_PWM2->PSEL.OUT[0] = (1 << PWM_PSEL_OUT_PORT_Pos) | (13 << PWM_PSEL_OUT_PIN_Pos);\n  26|   NRF_PWM2->PSEL.OUT[1] = PWM_PSEL_OUT_CONNECT_Disconnected << PWM_PSEL_OUT_CONNECT_Pos;\n  27|   NRF_PWM2->PSEL.OUT[2] = PWM_PSEL_OUT_CONNECT_Disconnected << PWM_PSEL_OUT_CONNECT_Pos;\n  28|   NRF_PWM2->PSEL.OUT[3] = PWM_PSEL_OUT_CONNECT_Disconnected << PWM_PSEL_OUT_CONNECT_Pos;\n  29| \n  30|   NRF_PWM2->ENABLE     = 1;\n  31|   NRF_PWM2->MODE       = PWM_MODE_UPDOWN_Up;\n  32|   NRF_PWM2->PRESCALER  = PWM_PRESCALER_PRESCALER_DIV_16;\n  33|   NRF_PWM2->COUNTERTOP = PWM_TOP_VAL;\n  34|   NRF_PWM2->LOOP       = 0;\n  35|   NRF_PWM2->DECODER    = PWM_DECODER_LOAD_Common | (PWM_DECODER_MODE_RefreshCount << PWM_DECODER_MODE_Pos);\n  36| \n  37|   NRF_PWM2->SEQ[0].PTR      = (uint32_t)seq_values;\n  38|   NRF_PWM2->SEQ[0].CNT      = 1;\n  39|   NRF_PWM2->SEQ[0].REFRESH  = 0;\n  40|   NRF_PWM2->SEQ[0].ENDDELAY = 0;\n  41| \n  42|   NRF_PWM2->TASKS_SEQSTART[0] = 1;\n  43| }\n  44| \n  45| void loop() {}\n</student_code>"
